commit 9ade0dc2882f837f9dfbea43e4665f3564a46c22
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Aug 19 18:24:53 2026 -0400

    Mark serial file boundaries on the wire
    
      Preserved per-file relay rows were triggered by any rise in the
      frame's files_done, but a fleet source's external counts rise on
      ordinary mid-flight ticks, painting a spurious row on every parallel
      run. The source now sets an HPNS_F_BOUNDARY flag on the frame it
      emits at a serial file completion, and the consumer preserves rows
      only for flagged frames. Fleet, verify, and resume-check meters never
      set the flag.

commit 0547751870709952677838130e2c124d5254d918
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Aug 19 18:11:08 2026 -0400

    Meter fixes from the visual test pass
    
      Parallel meters paint their final 100% line through the reporter
      instead of skipping it when the display is bound to another thread.
      Transfer-path messages print through a guard that clears a live meter
      line first, so text no longer lands glued to it. The verified resume
      re-fetch runs under its own transfer meter instead of stalling the
      resume-check meter, without double counting files. The relay meter
      renders the source's per-file progress: each completed file leaves a
      100% row with its own bytes, average and peak rates, and elapsed time,
      and the live line tracks the current file. The source now counts a
      file before emitting its boundary frame and forces the emit past the
      rate limiter, so the consumer sees each file's exact end state.

commit a2628405499d2b30422a1adcb0208aae11b8866e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 19:38:15 2026 -0400

    Remove the PTRACE pending-counter instrumentation
    
      The trace existed to pair pending increments and decrements when a ledger
      leak presents as a teardown hang. Its one real audition was this week's
      hang, where it went unused: gdb on the hung process found the cause
      directly, and the trace could not have, since the ledger was correct and
      the fault was elsewhere. Meanwhile the ledger it watched has become far
      easier to trust, with every mutation behind pending_mu in one helper, the
      wakeup edges tested, and ThreadSanitizer clean over the paths.
    
      Seventy odd lines of single purpose scaffolding, a parameter threaded
      through the give-up helpers, and two lock acquisitions on the requeue
      path all serviced that one debug line. Removed. This supersedes the
      previous commit's under-lock capture, which made the trace truthful just
      before it became clear the trace was not earning its keep.

commit b32fdf9ccfb0018d4304f9b7828ee11dccb6162c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 19:21:04 2026 -0400

    Capture the pending count under its lock for the PTRACE trace
    
      The pending-counter trace read p->pending in its debug argument list with
      no lock, racing the locked increments and decrements it exists to pair
      up. The count is now captured inside the critical section at each traced
      operation and passed to the trace, so the printed value is the ledger at
      that operation rather than whatever an unlocked read raced to. The
      requeue traces, which change no count, take the lock briefly to read a
      coherent level.
    
      This is just to support accuracy ina debug_3 statement and could likely
      be removed with no issue if we want to give up that debug.

commit 43cf4fd9cc441c684afcff7901b9f814ff97ad3b
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 19:07:18 2026 -0400

    Progress meter core: pin the fleet fill to the reporter
    
      A TSan pass caught some issues missed during prior work. The reporter was
      the only thread updating the fleet meter, but the fill runs on whatever
      thread drives refresh, and workers drive it on every received message.
      The fleet meter is now bound to the reporter's thread; refresh from any
      other thread leaves it alone. The bound check runs before the alarm flag
      is consumed, since a worker eating the tick left the meter silent.
    
      Also from the pass: sig_alarm preserves errno, and the alarm flag moves
      to relaxed atomics because threads read and clear it, not only the
      handler.

commit cb8d00f33efff13a91c5ae67210cfc29d6358688
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 17:11:29 2026 -0400

    rogress meter core: retire the singleton
    
      refresh_progress_meter now supplies only the shared cadence gates and
      routes to the core; the legacy fill is gone. start_progress_meter and
      stop_progress_meter become two line wrappers over a FILE meter, kept so
      upstream code arriving in a rebase still links and behaves; no in-tree
      caller uses them. The singleton state they served, the file and counter
      pointers, the position and rate globals, pm_set_total, and a dead
      aggregate_bytes_for_meter store are all deleted.
    
      TTY arming now gates on stdout being a terminal rather than on being in
      the foreground at start, so a transfer started in the background arms the
      resize handler and paints after fg instead of never painting. Painting
      itself stays foreground-gated. This will likely help if this ever gets a
      gui.
    
      progressmeter.c is left with the stock primitives, the shared render, the
      sink dispatch, the display session mechanics, and the debug capture. This
      removes a lot of the cruft we added to progressmeter.c.

commit 4f8f7598792311ad1f55197d9702e8c8646ce8b7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 17:03:21 2026 -0400

    Progress meter core: the fleet and relay sources, stage 5
    
      The parallel aggregate converts to a fleet meter on the core. The walker
      no longer touches display state: when an enumeration drains it posts the
      total and file count into atomic accumulators, and the reporter, now the
      only thread that updates the meter, folds them in on its next tick and
      rewrites the deferred-count label. The shared label buffer the walker and
      reporter both wrote is gone with it as per design. Posted totals
      are additive, so a second command grows a live denominator instead of
      replacing it and pinning the meter at a false 100 percent,
      and the deferred-count verb arms only when a start actually starts a
      meter, so a later command cannot rewrite a running meter's label or total.
      The resume-check stretch and the parallel verify meter convert to
      owner-checked core calls.
    
      The relay becomes a source in the core rather than a mode in
      progressmeter.c: the meter owns its label, a source-specific fill feeds
      the shared dispatch, and relay_mode, the placeholder counter wiring, and
      the relay branches in refresh and stop are deleted. Both consumers, scp -R
      and hpn3scp, move to the new entry points. Relay views now appear in the
      HPN_PM_DEBUG capture, which they never did before.

commit e6e85de6c12ed1f1ed683d78c08d9e5bed5ab3bd
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 16:25:57 2026 -0400

    Progress meter core: the bundle meter, stage 4
    
      The serial bundle flush converts to the core as a BUNDLE meter declaring
      its member count at start. The frame side's per-meter file flag becomes a
      per-meter file count, so a bundle of N files adds N to the frame stream's
      files_done instead of 1.
    
      The meter also starts for a bundle of only zero length files, which
      previously showed no meter and counted nothing. The kind renders the zero
      total as complete and the members count.

commit 812ab089cf99c79a1057568c2ed9289136f31d56
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 16:11:09 2026 -0400

    Progress meter core: the work sources
    
      The verify pass and the resume check convert to the meter core as WORK
      meters, verify in the work-byte domain (2x the moved bytes, both ends
      hash) and the resume check in the hash-byte domain.
    
      The core now derives the frame-side declarations from the kind and domain
      at start: a WORK meter is not a file, and its domain raises the resume or
      verify phase flag. The callers previously declared both after the meter
      was already running, which is the ordering that miscounted the bundle
      path.

