commit 26a50683296df0d5c29560f52224d4f52352b3fb
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Aug 18 15:18:14 2026 -0400

    Progress meter core: meters own their state, stage 1
    
      First stage of the meter rework.
      The old model hands progressmeter.c a borrowed label and counter through one
      interface that fits only the simplest caller, and five open review findings
      trace back to that.
    
      New unit hpn-meter.c: a meter owns a copy of its label, declares what it
      measures when it starts (kind, byte domain, and file count), and only the
      owner that started it may stop it. Its storage lives in the unit, so
      nothing dangles into a caller's dead stack frame. progressmeter.c keeps
      the display session mechanics (alarm, TTY arming, log guard, and sink
      dispatch) behind three new pm_ entry points, and the stock formatting
      primitives stay untouched.
    
      The three sftp per-file sites (download, upload, and crossload) convert to
      the core. Everything else stays on the legacy path, which coexists with
      the core behind a routing check in refresh_progress_meter.
    
      Debug-capture output is field-identical to the previous build for
      download and upload. One deliberate change: a zero byte file renders
      complete instead of a permanent 0 percent, since the FILE kind
      disambiguates a zero total from an unknown one. A resumed transfer's
      first sample confirms the offset seeding.

commit f07f07247b0b75aa2b7add794d03640c1604dcb8
