commit b8736b6823d8114f370a9748f6b7700239caef36
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Aug 16 16:34:47 2026 -0400

    Log a walked directory the way sftp-server always has
    
      sftp-server's OPENDIR handler has written logit("opendir \"%s\"") since
      2006, and administrators read it. The discover-tree walk enumerates a whole
      subtree server-side without it, logging only at debug3, so a recursive get
      produced one line per directory against a stock server and nothing against
      an HPN one. A client picks its walk from what the server advertises, so the
      same command against the same configuration lost a record that had been
      there for two decades.
    
      Emit the same string, at the same level, before the open, so it means a
      listing was requested rather than that one succeeded. Per directory, since
      that is the volume anything parsing this already expects.

commit 1196ea1dca356abc292181285c1a43fa9b6e4358
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Aug 16 16:21:06 2026 -0400

    Latch the bundling knobs before scp's single-stream returns
    
      scp_parallel_launch resolved HPNUseBundle, HPNBundleSize and HPNWriterPool
      and latched them on the control connection, but did so below two early
      returns taken when there is no orchestrator: parallel_num_streams <= 1,
      which is the default, and eff_streams <= 1 after the server's worker cap.
      Without -j the serial recursive walks run on that connection and are the
      only readers of these, so the case the call exists for was the one case it
      could not reach. The connection kept the compile-time defaults, and -o
      HPNUseBundle=no, -o HPNWriterPool=no and HPNBundleSize were silently
      ignored.
    
      Resolve and latch above the returns, into a scratch config, mirroring how
      sftp.c already does it. The parallel path still resolves the same options
      into its own pcfg.

commit d5e78dc4c68e8e4dfc48b364ace09e172a45e344
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 17:46:30 2026 -0400

    Honour -f for bundled downloads
    
      sftp_hpn_bundle_download took no fsync argument. Its writer pool was
      constructed with the flag hardcoded off, and the serial extract path never
      flushed at all, so get -f routed every file under 8 MiB into a bundle and
      synced none of them while reporting success. Only files above the bundle
      ceiling honoured the flag. The upload half of the same accumulator threads
      it through, so this was an omission rather than a bundle limitation.
    
      Thread the flag to both paths. The pool already supported it and only
      needed to be told; the serial path now fsyncs before the close, and treats
      a failure as a real error, since -f exists so a caller can trust the data
      is on disk when the command succeeds.
    
      The parallel worker path had never honoured -f either and is fixed here
      too, so both bundle download paths behave the same.

commit 6aa741b021157e8e067dfbf953e19f1bcd2414ca
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 15:17:01 2026 -0400

    Mark the connection dead when a discover-tree reply cannot be parsed
    
      Three bail-outs in the fetch loop logged and returned without calling
      sftp_conn_die: the reply header, chunk header and record parses. A message
      we cannot decode leaves the stream desynced, and the server keeps sending
      chunks to its END regardless. Those chunks then surfaced as an id mismatch
      on whatever command ran next, which failed carrying the wrong path. Every
      neighbouring exit in the same loop already died for this reason. The
      transport failures need no change, since send_msg and get_msg_extended
      already mark the connection, and a STATUS reply is a complete response.
    
      The loop also ended only when the server said END. Refusing an empty chunk
      already stopped a peer spinning us on chunks carrying nothing, so what
      remained was an endless stream of real ones: work on every record, so never
      idle and never finished. Cap the records one enumeration may deliver. It is
      a backstop rather than a security boundary, since a hostile server can
      exhaust a client with a genuinely large tree either way, and it is set far
      above any real tree so reaching it means the peer is lying.

commit 8220dcd91562f022ad47af15354b501ed9dc8dc6
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 14:01:58 2026 -0400

    Apply deferred directory attrs deepest first
    
      sftp_hpn_dirattrs_apply walked the list in insertion order, and its comment
      asserted that order was children before parents. That holds only for the
      upload driver, which defers after recursing. Every download and third-party
      sink defers inside make_dir, and the discover-tree wire contract emits
      parents before children, so for those the order was reversed.
    
      A directory is created with owner write and execute forced on so the
      transfer can write into it, and the deferred attrs put the real mode back.
      Applying a parent first can strip the owner execute bit its children still
      need, and every chmod and utimes below it then fails, leaving those children
      with the widened mode the deferral exists to undo. Reaching that needs the
      final mode to lack owner execute while the source was still walkable, so it
      takes a more privileged server or a directory traversable by group alone.
    
      Sort deepest first before applying, so the result no longer depends on which
      producer built the list, and correct both comments that described the old
      invariant.

commit e35422d970d49d82977eb46f9f40b5792165a0c2
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 13:48:03 2026 -0400

    Fail an upload whose remote mkdir failed
    
      sftp_upload_walk_consume discarded sftp_mkdir_pipeline's return, so a
      directory that could not be created on the remote left ret unset and
      sink->fail uncalled. An empty subdirectory transfers nothing, so nothing
      else noticed the destination was missing and the command exited 0. With -p
      it was worse: the walk still descended and deferred the directory's
      attributes, and the setstat then applied them to whatever regular file
      occupied that path.
    
      The count alone does not say which directory failed, so the pipeline now
      reports that per path. The caller fails the transfer, records each path
      through sink->fail so the parallel summary sees it, and does not descend
      into a directory that does not exist. That matches what upload_dir_internal
      did before the walk was consolidated.

commit 053c36936e2c8e94b5c5a0e01bc3996df7a92187
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 12:34:05 2026 -0400

    Validate peer paths against the same separators the readdir walk uses
    
      sftp_tree_relpath_ok splits on '/' alone. Cygwin's runtime resolves a
      backslash as a separator, so "..\..\..\etc\passwd" passed as one component
      that is not "..", and the client opened it O_WRONLY|O_CREAT three levels
      above the transfer root. The readdir walk has always split on
      SFTP_DIRECTORY_CHARS, which is "/\\" there for exactly this reason, so a
      Cygwin client was protected against a hostile server on one path and not
      the other. Inert on POSIX, where a backslash is an ordinary filename byte
      and must stay legal.
    
      Nothing bounded the length either. A bundled download lists every member's
      path in one request whose budget reserves a single PATH_MAX of overshoot,
      so one longer path made that request unsendable and killed the client with
      a message blaming the remote shell.
    
      Split on the platform's separator set, bound the path at PATH_MAX, and
      reject "." and empty components, which the header already claimed were
      rejected and were not.

commit 509ed6a786e17907467fc2fa44b06754d1742ad9
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 12:12:13 2026 -0400

    Report the failures the discover-tree walk was swallowing
    
      The walk moved from the client to the server, and the error reporting the
      readdir walk had did not move with it.
    
      The depth cap returned bare, so a tree past 64 levels lost everything below
      that: the stream still ended normally, the client copied what it was told
      about and exited 0, no diagnostic. readdir returns NULL at end of directory
      and on error alike, so a failure part way through read as a short directory.
      A failure of the walk root is encoded with an empty relative path, but the
      client validated the path before dispatching on record type, so an
      unreadable root was reported as the peer sending a suspect path rather than
      as permission denied.
    
      All three now emit or handle an error record, which reaches the client arm
      that fails the transfer.
    
      The cap was defined four times at 64, which is how the two walks drifted.
      The three HPN copies collapse to one. Upstream's is left alone to avoid a
      rebase conflict, and sftp-client.c now fails the build if they diverge.

commit c2012a97f223d006e9a117e84c8a32bcc6ecd8dc
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 15 00:17:23 2026 -0400

    Wake the capacity wait when room actually frees
    
        The pending counter signalled only its zero transition. That is what
        sftp_parallel_wait needs, but a producer throttled by
        sftp_parallel_await_capacity waits on a different edge: the point where
        pending drops below the outstanding ceiling, reached long before zero.
        That signal never came, so every capacity wait fell out on its own
        timeout instead.
    
        Signal that point too, and only that point: signalling every decrement
        below the ceiling would broadcast once per completed file for nothing.
        The two push-fail paths open-coded the decrement and signalled nothing,
        which is how they drifted, so all three sites now share one helper.
    
        The timeout was meant as a backstop and had become the mechanism, pacing
        the walk at one ceiling per timeout period rather than at the rate the
        fleet drains. On a slower filesystem this wasn't an issue but on faster
        filesystems this could end up imposing a real hit. Measured at ~17% in
        our local tests. This is because the transfer fleet was idled while the
        walker slept.

commit aee7d75d539227875d280725884dfcd33741cd25
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Aug 14 23:12:03 2026 -0400

    Bound the files a discover-tree walk leaves outstanding
    
        The walk enumerates far faster than the fleet transfers, so it submitted
        the whole tree before much of it had moved and every outstanding file's
        unit and its two path strings sat in memory at once.  The workqueue's
        depth cap did not stop that.  The cap counts queued objects, and under
        bundling one object is a bundle carrying thousands of files, so it sat
        orders of magnitude above what it was sized to allow and never engaged.
        In our test case this was upwards of 420MB of memory in use for this.
    
        Block the walk on the outstanding FILE count instead, which is the one
        quantity meaningful for both a bundle and a file too large to bundle.
        The ceiling comes from the maximum a bundle can hold rather than an
        assumed occupancy: real packing varies with the byte target, the member
        cap and the download path-list limit, and a ceiling derived from any one
        of them starves the others.  Against the maximum every worker keeps its
        two bundles whatever the packing, so the ceiling bounds memory without
        becoming the thing that limits throughput.  Blocking here stops the walk
        reading the reply, so back-pressure reaches the server. This reduced memory
        to a nax of about 55MB abd provided a small performance imporvement as well.

commit 688039e14df2214d9062c8b63fcdf1328cdabcd0
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Aug 12 20:14:04 2026 -0400

    Stream discovered files to the fleet instead of queueing the tree
    
        The parallel download walk collected every regular file into a growing
        array and submitted the batch only after the discover-tree stream had
        drained. Each entry costs 56 bytes of record plus two heap path
        strings, held for the whole tree before a single byte moves, so the
        client's walk footprint grew with the file count without bound. For
        absurdly large file trees this could be a signficiant delay and memory
        sink.
    
        Submit each file as its record arrives instead. The worker queue it
        feeds is sized from -j alone, so a five million file tree now costs the
        same walk memory as a small one. A sink opts in with streams_files.
        Parallel download qualifies because submitting only enqueues work for
        the fleet. Serial and crossload transfer on the connection that is
        streaming, so they leave it clear and keep the deferred queue. The
        meter's total and count are tallied as records arrive, since the file
        array stays empty for a streaming sink. 3rd party should work as well
        as each endpoint supports it.
    
        One behaviour change. When the worker queue fills, the walker blocks in
        submit and stops reading the reply, so back-pressure reaches the server
        and discovery is paced by transfer. That is what keeps the memory
        bounded. Workers drain the queue independently of the walker, so it
        cannot deadlock. The impact of this hasn't been fully explored as of
        yet but I don't forsee any major issues.

commit 113ec5f7089bb4d9a02f8eb13f1acb8de5d484b3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Aug 7 23:47:16 2026 -0400

    Refuse a second request while a streamed reply is in flight
    
      The client matches a reply to its request by reading the next message in
      wire order, so a request sent while a multi-message reply is still
      arriving has its reply interleaved with the remaining chunks: one reader
      consumes a message meant for the other and both desynchronise. This collision
      was causing the interleaved directory/file discovery process to fail.
      That causes a "reply id mismatch" failure that the first attempt that
      interleave hit. Also it is timing-dependent so it surfaces late and intermittently.
    
      Three places already maintain this rule by hand: the deferred file
      submission in the discover-tree walk, the mkdir pipeline's two-phase
      split, and the setstat pipeline's shape. However, nothing enforced it.
      Instead we mark the connection while a streamed reply drains and
      have send_msg fail there, so a violation stops at the offending site
      instead of corrupting the stream. Scope is limited to one connection.

commit 731560b4bd7c17c1817675820a0f6f803b5c9824
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Aug 7 09:17:08 2026 -0400

    Refuse a swapped symlink when opening a walk directory
    
      The discover-tree walk classified an entry with lstat and then opened it
      by path, so the name could be replaced with a symlink in between and the
      enumeration would follow it out of the requested tree. Open with
      O_NOFOLLOW below the root when the client asked not to follow symlinks,
      so a replaced final component fails instead of redirecting the walk. The
      root is client-named and a follow walk resolves links, so neither is
      guarded. Platforms without fdopendir keep the plain open, matching
      OpenSSH's own OPENDIR handler. This is very unlikely but it is correct
      behaviour.
    
      Upstream resolves OPENDIR the same way, so this hardens the discover-tree
      path only. The readdir fallback runs against servers that lack the
      extension and is unchanged.

commit 292a0696b3bdc95c8aadf104452f1f4c03333384
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Aug 6 16:02:36 2026 -0400

    Four unbounded paths found in a branch review.
    
      The server flushed a chunk after 256 records with no byte bound, so a
      tree with long relative paths produced an EXTENDED_REPLY over
      SFTP_MAX_MSG_LENGTH and the client took its fatal path. Bound the chunk
      by bytes too, reserving the header plus one full-length record.
    
      A followed symlink resolving back onto the walk path was descended
      again, costing k^depth traversals in one uninterruptible request. Track
      (dev, ino) per depth and emit an ERROR record instead.
    
      An interrupt was noticed per record but never stopped the drain, so a
      large enumeration ignored it until the server finished. A non-zero
      callback return now stops decoding and discards the rest unread, still
      reading to the END marker so the connection stays in sync. A DATA chunk
      carrying no records is refused.
    
      Peer-supplied sizes were summed into a signed off_t with no guard, so a
      large total wrapped negative and the meter rendered nonsense. Clamp per
      entry and on the sum, falling back to a rate-only meter, and treat a
      negative total as unknown in pm_set_total.

commit 7137a68b3606ccff9acb8dc32e85c3de3740ae0e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Aug 3 12:27:59 2026 -0400

    Parallel download progress misreported count, total, and summary
    
      Three inaccuracies in the -j download progress output.
    
      A single-file source in sftp started the meter with total 0, so it
      showed bytes and a rate but no percentage or ETA. process_get now stats
      a lone regular-file source and sizes the meter to it, as scp and
      process_put already do.
    
      A directory download labelled the meter "Fetching 1 file in parallel"
      because the count came from the command line, where a directory is one
      glob match. The real count is not known until the discover-tree walk
      drains, so defer it: start a count-less "<verb> files in parallel" and
      rewrite it to "<verb> N files in parallel" once the walk delivers the
      count. sftp keeps "Fetching", scp keeps "Downloading". Uploads scan the
      local tree, so their count was always right and is untouched.
    
      The "Parallel streams: ... transferred" line was a session-lifetime
      rollup but printed after every command, so an interactive user watched
      it climb after each get. Emit it only on the final drain and label it
      "session total".

commit 893b33536bb41e640b2935b308251912b38244bb
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Aug 2 11:54:59 2026 -0400

    Serial bundle downloads showed no progress meter
    
      Small files in a serial directory download are batched into a bundle and
      never reach sftp_download, where the per-file meter lives, so they moved
      with no meter at all. Meter each bundle flush as one unit. Thread an
      optional received-byte counter into sftp_hpn_bundle_download, bumped in
      the data callback, and have the serial download flush wrap it in an
      "N files" meter. Non-bundled files keep their per-file meters, and the
      parallel workers pass NULL so their aggregate meter is untouched.
    
      I'm not entirely happy with this methods but the parallelism makes this
      difficult and I don't want to spend that much more time dealing with a
      UI fix. I'm starting to think I need to completely rework how a
      progressmeter works in parallel mode. It might be necessart to have a
      parallel progressmeter as well as a serial one. Which would suck but it
      terms of it feeding an eventual GUI might be necessary.

commit e76a28806a63abc798918b753e2edbec497afa26
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 23:44:01 2026 -0400

    Parallel downloads never showed a percentage or ETA
    
      A parallel directory download's meter was started without the
      directory's total size, so it had nothing to measure completion against
      and never showed a percentage or an ETA. A single-file source written
      as "~/..." was left the same way for a different reason: the stat that
      would size the meter ran on the raw path, which does not resolve in a
      bare SFTP stat.
    
      For the directory case, the discover-tree enumeration already carries
      every file's size and queues every file before the transfer starts. Sum
      those sizes when the stream drains and update the running meter with
      pm_set_total. Only the parallel download sink implements the new
      set_total hook. Serial and third-party sinks leave it NULL, and the
      readdir fallback against a stock server has no complete total.
    
      For the tilde case, run that pre-transfer stat through
      prepare_remote_path, the same canonicalization the transfer uses, so a
      "~/..." source resolves and yields a total. Both changes touch only the
      parallel progress path.

commit d1655bb5e109f37b7a0c7a5d036396b76a3a1f2d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 22:51:15 2026 -0400

    Render an unknown (0) total progress meter as rate-only
    
      A parallel directory download starts its progress meter with an
      unknown (0) total, and the refresh math read that 0 as
      "transfer complete" on every tick — zeroing the rate,
      reporting  a false stall, and freezing the bar at 100%. So a
      transfer that was actively moving data displayed as
      100% / 0.0KB/s / - stalled -
      instead of showing its real  throughput.
    
      Treat total 0 as an unknown total instead: keep the real delta
      and rate, stall only when bytes actually stop, and read 0%
      rather than 100%. Every new branch is gated on end_pos == 0,
      so the known-total path is unchanged.

commit 845c8dd2b57d80704d3c5c0bf80a6d65d612cfdb
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 18:52:45 2026 -0400

    Add HPN_PM_DEBUG for capturing progress-meter state without a TTY.
    
      The meter suppresses itself on a non-TTY, so its computed values are only
      visible on a terminal, which makes meter behavior hard to review. Add a
      developer-only env var HPN_PM_DEBUG. Set it to a file path (or "1"/"-" for
      stderr) and the meter runs and writes one text line per refresh with the
      values it computed (label, cur, total, percent, rate, inst, stalled, eta,
      elapsed), regardless of TTY.
    
      progressmeter.c gets the text sink plus the wiring to run and dump when the
      var is set while still skipping the TTY paint on a pipe. scp.c and sftp.c
      stop forcing showprogress off on a non-TTY when the var is set, so the
      meter starts. Client-only, and inert unless the var is set.

commit 6a681c69ebdcba167815fcf514a33b63fdb80852
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 18:15:02 2026 -0400

    Defer parallel file submission until the discover-tree stream drains.
    
      The streaming refactor submitted each file to the worker fleet inline
      during the discover-tree enumeration. That submit issues a request on the
      control connection while the discover-tree reply is still arriving, so they
      collide and the fetch reads a reply with the wrong id ("hpn-discover-tree
      reply id mismatch"), killing the connection. Only parallel is affected
      (serial and -j1 already deferred), and it is timing-dependent, so an
      earlier parallel run passed and it surfaced later.
    
      Make parallel defer too. All modes now queue files during enumeration and
      transfer them after the stream drains, so nothing touches the control
      connection mid-stream. Directories still stream inline, and with deferral
      universal the defer_file_xfer flag and inline branch are removed.

commit e5c9d13c74dac1cd758f41f151f3dd66f6ad8dae
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 12:53:52 2026 -0400

    Stream the discover-tree download instead of buffering the whole tree.
    
      sftp_hpn_discover_tree drained the entire enumeration into an array before
      returning, so the download consumer could not act until the full tree had
      arrived. Make the fetch invoke a per-record callback as records stream off
      the wire, and drive the download from it. This brings it into the same format
      as the upload side.
    
      Directories are created inline as their records arrive; the wire contract
      emits a parent before its children, so a dir's parent exists when its
      record is seen. Regular files transfer inline when the sink runs on its
      own connection (parallel workers, defer_file_xfer=0), so discovery and
      transfer overlap. When the transfer shares the stream's connection (serial
      download and crossload, defer_file_xfer=1) files are queued and moved
      after the stream drains, since the server push stream holds the connection
      until END.

commit 8e4683d4e6b9e33ee9b568cd7deb060cb70a0ff3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Aug 1 09:39:30 2026 -0400

    Follow symlinks in the discover-tree path when the client asks.
    
      scp passes follow_link_flag=1 and expects recursive copies to follow
      symlinks. The discover-tree download path ignored the flag and always
      skipped them because sftp ignores them. Thsi meant scp over a
      discover-tree server dropped symlinked entries.
    
      Thread follow_link_flag through the path. sftp_tree_download_consume sets
      the FOLLOW_SYMLINKS request bit, and the three download entry points
      forward their flag. When the bit is set the server walk stats a symlink's
      target and treats the entry as that target. A directory target is
      descended, a regular one is emitted as REG, a broken link emits an ERROR.
      Loops are bounded by the depth cap, the same as the readdir path.
    
      This does *not* enable symlinks in sftp as they aren't supported upstream
      as of yet.

commit ed204228c6e0875009a28ac015ef0bdf6ef2194b
