commit 728758f329f506968a79f6c3e83278f932478eb4
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jul 31 17:13:34 2026 -0400

    Add the hpn-discover-tree server walk and shared record codec.
    
      Server side of the remote directory-discovery extension. The handler
      walks a subtree locally (recursive opendir/readdir) and push-streams it
      back as chunked SSH2_FXP_EXTENDED_REPLY records, draining each chunk via
      flush_oqueue_blocking so a large tree never buffers in memory. Directory
      records precede their contents (the wire ordering contract); symlinks are
      emitted but not followed, matching OpenSSH's recursive transfers; "." and
      ".." are excluded; an unreadable subdirectory emits an inline ERROR
      record and the walk continues; depth is capped at HPN_DTREE_MAX_DEPTH which
      is the same at MAX_DIR_DEPTH defined as 64 in sftp-client.c
    
      The direction-neutral record codec (sftp-hpn-tree.c) is shared with the
      client consumer to come. No transfer behavior changes yet - nothing
      invokes the extension until the client fetch is wired into the download
      walks.

commit 8962fad7d3360ab172ca9e178583592cbb688958
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jul 31 16:26:40 2026 -0400

    Scaffold the hpn-discover-tree extension (negotiate + advertise).
    
      The goal of this work is to reduce the amoutn of time it takes to
      discover the tree structure of remote filesystems when we are doing
      downloads. This process is largely serial and imposes the same
      delay of #dirs * RTT (usually multiple RTTS). By batching the
      directory structure we can improve throughput by spending less time
      blocked by RTT dependent queries.
    
      Wire contract (sftp-hpn-tree.h), client SFTP_EXT bit + negotiation
      clause, and server advertisement with a stub dispatch that falls
      through to OP_UNSUPPORTED. No behavior change; the streamed server
      walk and client consumer follow.

commit 483569189cb1f0e23a07e3a4e32b566ac2f429b3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jul 31 14:27:23 2026 -0400

    Pipeline recursive-upload directory creation (mkdir batch).
    
      The recursive upload walk issued one blocking SSH2_FXP_MKDIR per
      directory resulting in a delay of #dir * RTT extra time.
      Collect a directory level's subdirs during the walk and create
      them as one pipelined, fully-drained batch (sftp_mkdir_pipeline, 64-deep
      window, chunked at MKDIR_BATCH_MAX) before recursing. Siblings are
      order-independent so batching is safe, and the full drain guarantees a
      directory exists before its files are written.
    
      Also, introduced a bug where we tried to call xalloc even if the
      nuber of directories was zero. This led to exit code 255. This was
      resolved by putting a guard in place to return if dl->n is zero.

commit 57507354c2e8f466659bed3c1cdace58e8005a58
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jul 30 22:27:46 2026 -0400

     Pipeline the deferred directory setstats.
    
      The deferred directory-attribute apply issued one blocking SETSTAT per
      directory - this could result in significant added time (# dir * rtt * 2)
      The solution: send a bounded window of outstanding requests and drain
      in order instead; replies come back in request order and directory
      setstats have no inter-directory dependency. Thsi benfits both the
      serial and parallel transfers.
    
      Also drops two unused variables left in parallel_upload_walk by the
      earlier directory-handling refactor.

commit 5f4dac026082b0927a88ffdf05529129a8115030
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jul 30 17:57:28 2026 -0400

    Documentation update in hpn-sftp-client.h
    
      Setting directory permissions are deferred until after the
      entire data set is transferred. The reason for this is because
      bundles can cross directory boundaries and timing when to
      set the permission becomes complicated with parallel workers.
      This is not an ideal solution because midtransfer failures
      can leave directories with unwanted permissions. File permissions
      are set with each write but unless we track the last file of any
      given directory we end up with this. This probably needs to be
      revisited.

commit 41512f3e5d651312e14d1a40080cf3586bdebe51
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jul 30 17:34:28 2026 -0400

    Defer setting directory permission and refactor.
    
      Stock OpenSSH applies a directory's final permissions after uploading
      its files (setstat runs at the end of upload_dir_internal, past the
      synchronous per-file loop), so a restrictive mode such as 0555 is set
      only once the directory no longer needs to be written. HPN's small-file
      bundling broke that: bundled files accumulate during the walk
      and are written when the bundle flushes, but the directory setstat still
      ran inline as the walk left the directory - applying perms before the
      data was written. On a read-only directory under -p this loses files.
      Record directory attributes during the walk and apply them after all
      data has landed - serial at the end of the recursive walk, parallel
      at the end of sftp_parallel_wait once every unit has drained.
    
      Consolidate the previously hand-copied mkdir / stat-fallback / attribute
      logic from the four upload and download walks (serial and parallel
      producer) into shared helpers in sftp-hpn-client.c, so both modes make
      identical directory decisions from one implementation.

