commit 078ad382c1a647bc8af68d5ff6db3c87e9acc1b7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jul 28 13:12:00 2026 -0400

    Harden upload pacing: continuous re-arm, down-step clamp, fast reclaim.
    
      Drop the per-file cold-arm; hundreds of small files per second would
      re-arm constantly, so the ceiling now re-arms mid-flow on a 2s cadence
      and behaves identically for any file mix. Bound each re-arm's cut to
      half the prior ceiling so one famine-polluted measurement window
      cannot crater a healthy ceiling into a slow x5/4 climb-back. When the
      sink again consumes >=90% of a famine-cut ceiling, jump back to 80% of
      the last rising-armed ceiling (the last proven mean delivery rate) and
      restart the estimator ring, halving the cost of a famine episode.
    
      We may need more work on making the recovery phase more aggressive.
      Yes, we are essentially rebuilding ssthresh here.

commit 00dbcfa1edad3beeba8b43b5d7e585c81eda0bac
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jul 27 16:13:00 2026 -0400

    Add adaptive upload pacing to the SFTP client.
    
      Single-stream uploads at high RTT into a destination filesystem slower
      than the path collapse into a stall/recover duty cycle. Unpaced ingest
      overfills the receiver's page cache to the file system's dirty-page cliff,
      the sink blocks for seconds while this is cleared, window credit starves,
      and every cycle pays an RTT-scaled TCP recovery. Pace WRITE issues to 5/4
      of the mean acked rate. This is a 10s window, first 3s of the connection
      excised to skip slow-start givng us an artifically low rate. This 3 second
      excise might need to be modified later. We do this pacing by repurposing
      the -l rate limit token bucket. Testing shows real gains in throughput for
      single and dual workers. More than that and even the decreased throughput
      in an unpaced worker gets covered up by the number of workers.
    
      Enabled by default; -X Pacing=no disables. This is a client side fix so
      it will work with older versions of HPN-SSH. This is an intentional design
      decision - having pacing measurements on both sides might be faster and
      ore accurate but we end up locking the client into HPN-19+.
    
      Yes, this is more flow control but the send side needs this at high
      throughput/RTT. This really became obvious when the RTT was around 100ms.

commit 50f31793c2f45c1ee626e438cd9b362471d41466
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jul 18 12:58:10 2026 -0400

    Consolidate the progress meter onto a shared meter_view
    
      refresh_progress_meter had a three-way fork after the rate math - relay
      render, frame emit, and TTY paint - each re-deriving the display numbers
      its own way, with the local and relay tail/percent logic duplicated and
      already drifting.
    
      Introduce struct meter_view as the single per-meter currency.  refresh now
      fills one view and dispatches to a sink: render_from_view (the sole TTY
      renderer, shared by the local meter and the relay consumer) or
      hpn_pm_emit_view (the frame sink, which overlays the cross-file aggregate
      and derives its per-second rate from the view's raw delta/elapsed).  The
      relay stops being a special renderer and becomes a view filler; its
      source-specific choices and the unknown-total percent policy
      live in the fill, not the shared renderer.

