commit 47ffb6b9efcf86a09fb5afe86f5b34b745c2ff0c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jul 13 15:03:52 2026 -0400

    Prepend bindir to user_path in configure.ac
    
      When multiple copies of scp/hpnscp are installed the sshd process
      will default to using the first one it's path. That's usually going to
      be /usr/bin and the /usr/local/bin. If it's been installed into a
      non-standard location (e.g. /opt/hpnssh) it puts the binary path for that
      installation last so it will rarely get used. Prepending the bindir path
      to the user path means it will look in the named installation diretcory
      first. This doesn't resolve issues with PAM rewriting the PATH or the user
      making PATH changes but it should cover the majority of issues.

commit 362dd4f22e4ed496da6483e7b5f84dab4b817303
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jul 12 23:08:43 2026 -0400

    Add zstd transport compression (zstd@hpnssh.org)
    
      A fast alternative to the transport's single-threaded zlib: zstd, at
      the same packet-path insertion points and the same delayed (post-auth)
      semantics. Each HPN worker is its own hpnssh session, so N workers give
      N parallel compressor pairs - the parallelism zlib never had.
    
      Client -z level (1-19) requests it; the client then proposes
      zstd@hpnssh.org alone, so a peer without support fails the KEX cleanly
      rather than silently degrading. -z and -C are mutually exclusive. The
      server offers the method whenever built with libzstd (no admin toggle,
      same as zlib) and compresses its own outbound direction at ZstdLevel
      (default 3); levels are per-direction-local. -z forwards through the
      parallel workers, scp -R, and hpn3scp.
    
      All zstd code is confined to hpn-compress.c/h. packet.c only dispatches
      on the negotiated type and the zlib path is untouched. libzstd is
      auto-detected in configure.ac (--with-zstd).
    
      Man pages and usage updated.

commit 9a2f5838e7f09a9b0ba12d6d506e3c25e5c618ac
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jul 12 17:17:58 2026 -0400

    Add a per-file transfer log (-oTransferLog) to all three clients
    
      -oTransferLog[=path] (default ./hpnssh-transfer.log) writes
      one tab-delimited line per file - status, size, path - and a run
      footer with totals and elapsed time. Statuses are success, skipped,
      verified, repaired, and failed; one word per file, most specific
      wins, written where the status is final (completion, or the verify
      resolution under -V, which now reports repaired vs verified). The
      option is consumed by the client, never passed to ssh. An unwritable
      target fails before any connection. Purely observational: nothing
      reads the log back. The log lives in append mode.
    
      A new FILEDONE frame carries each file's status, size, and opaque
      path when the source is armed with the "log" env value - the per-file
      event stream the relay design deferred in July. hpn3scp and the
      hpnscp -R launcher consume it and write the log on the launching
      host, percent-encoding remote path bytes before they land in a
      displayable file. hpn3scp also emits EVENT file_status for
      front-ends. Old sources degrade to header and footer. Fuzz harness
      covers the new decoder. Man pages updated.

commit 772f93f4efa7f977190af2cbe7b8c1cb602d179f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jul 12 15:42:30 2026 -0400

    Parallelize verified-resume refill via submit-time splitting
    
      A -Z resume of a large partial ran everything through one worker: hash
      both ends on one connection, then re-send every missing run single-
      stream while the rest of the fleet idled - on fast paths a resumed
      500GB file lost badly to just re-sending it.
    
      At submit, a partial destination now splits at its EOF under one range
      tracker: the tail past dest EOF is known missing and
      goes straight to ordinary range units, unhashed; the overlap submits as
      RESUME_SPAN units whose executor is the shared verify+repair engine
      (repair forced on - for resume the repair is the transfer), so hashing
      and splicing run concurrently across the fleet. All units are built in
      the submit thread; completion, verify parking, retries, and respawn
      recovery are the ordinary range-split semantics. Whole-file fallback
      remains for absent/equal/larger destinations, missing extensions, and
      sub-split files. Serial paths unchanged because they are serial.

commit a4658379e299f8e18379f9e9868fcaafcdc22475
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jul 11 23:31:52 2026 -0400

    Disable verify, resume, and parallelism for scp -3.
    
      -3 enables scp's crossload modeality which transfer files
      from A to C through the client host B. Prior to this these
      optiosn were being accepted but not actually passed along
      to the client processes. The were being silently swallowed.
    
      I don't think this is a frequently used feature and adding
      these hpn specific features is added complexity for little
      potential benefit. As such, we just don't support them at
      this time. If users demonstrate a need for this we can get
      back to this later.

