commit 68fb30f6b513b16e535d7f5aa1012d0184943b4f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jul 11 17:41:47 2026 -0400

    Meter hash phases in work-bytes; feed both legs everywhere
    
      Unified hash-progress accounting. Every hash phase; -Z resume check,
      -V verify, auto-repair, &c meters in work-bytes, where checking one
      byte of overlap costs two (one local, one remote).  Totals are 2x the
      span, BOTH legs feed one monotone counter (done = leg base +
      leg progress), and the displayed rate is each leg's true hashing
      throughput.  Consistent across every meter surface: serial
      and parallel, hpnscp and hpnsftp, and the -R/hpn3scp relay. Note this
      means that we have a single meter that measure both sides of the hash
      so a 20GB data set will create 40GB of hashing for the meter. If this
      ends up being a difficult UX decision we can work on that later.
    
      One conn-level accounting pair (hash_work_done/total + liveness stamp)
      with begin/leg/progress/end accessors replaces the three overlapping
      signals. The engines drive it: the chunked span, the whole-file
      hash gates (per-site leg order), and the verify core whose local
      read-back now feeds through the previously-unused progress callback.
      Completion sites capture-end-fold in that order. The serial verify
      meter gains the bridge, so a single-file -V moves smoothly through both
      legs instead of jumping 0->100 at the end.
    
      Also fix sftp_parallel_progress_stop: its completion snapshot painted
      transfer bytes onto whatever meter was live, which lands at 50% on a
      work-byte verify meter (and was only accidentally 100% before); snap to
      the live meter's own total instead.

