commit 320ce1c89a7c42865896ed83c171642f11c77122
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jul 10 16:56:58 2026 -0400

    Cancel parallel transfers when the controlling session dies
    
      ^C on a -R launcher never reached the source: no signal crosses the ssh
      hop (the dying client sends none; sshd refuses session signals and never
      kills no-tty children), and parallel mode ignores SIGPIPE - so the
      source ran the whole transfer as an orphan, unsupervised writes on live
      authenticated connections the operator believed were stopped.
    
      Detect session death at the transport layer: the reporter tick polls
      stdout AND stderr (no I/O); both dead means sshd-session exited with our
      session, so abort - exactly like a local interrupt. One dead pipe alone
      (stdout piped to a reader that exited) keeps stock pass-through
      behavior. Works with the display fully disabled (-q, no meter, no
      frames): the progressmeter displays data and carries no control - its
      frame writer now merely latches EPIPE to stop writing to a dead pipe.
    
      An aborted run also exits nonzero now (new sftp_parallel_was_aborted;
      scp reports "transfer aborted") instead of claiming success over a
      deliberately incomplete destination.
    
      Serial sources keep stock behavior (SIGPIPE on next write). Survivable
      transfers remain available the established way: run the launcher under
      tmux/nohup so the whole supervised session persists.

commit d071b18aaab5a96176831f38e25ff3c15e889634
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jul 10 16:23:53 2026 -0400

    Exempt hashing workers from watchdog kills; clamp resume checks at dest EOF
    
      A worker running a resume check / verify / repair hash is byte-silent by
      design. The watchdog pause covers the per-tick classifiers while its
      lease is held, but nothing covered the throughput-streak accumulators in
      watchdog_sample_throughput - ticks earned across a long hash could fire
      the kill moments after the hash ended - nor the tail between the engine's
      watchdog_resume and the first post-hash bytes. Gate on the conn's hash-op
      marker (heartbeat-refreshed, self-clears ~3s after the engine stops):
      born-dead / tput-outlier / born-slow skip a marked worker, and the streak
      counters reset instead of accumulating. Hard evidence (child exit) is
      unaffected, and a wedged hash stops refreshing the marker, so the reaper
      re-arms within seconds.
    
      Chunked resume also hashed the full source chunk map even when the
      destination held only a fraction of the file: chunks extending past the
      destination's EOF cannot match. Clamp the check to the dest extent
      (4x less hashing for a 5GB partial of a 20GB file), send the tail
      straight to the refill list, and report hashed vs known-missing chunks
      distinctly in the completion logit. dest_size=0 (repair path, equal-size
      resumes) preserves the full check.

