commit 9993af646820a70d935d660602fab72eb875df2e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 8 11:53:24 2026 -0400

    hpn3scp Stage 2: host-key trust broker + -4/-6
    
      Establish trust in the target's host key before the workers connect,
      instead of relying on the source's implicit accept-new. See
      hpn-launcher-design.md sec 9.
    
      New module hpn3scp-hostkey.c:
      Fetch: the source ssh-keyscans the target; a strict, remote-facing
      parser turns the result into validated sshkeys (rejects
      @cert-authority/@revoked markers and malformed keys, skips comments).
      Fingerprints are computed by us from the parsed key, never taken from
      the source's text. SSHFP classification (verify_host_key_dns):
      absent/match/secure/mismatch. We don't expect SSHFP to be widely deployed
      but it's good practice to have it available.
    
      Verification ladder, fail-closed:
      Source already trusts the target (ssh-keygen -F) -> skip brokering;
      DNSSEC-secure SSHFP match -> auto-accept;
      SSHFP mismatch -> abort (possible MITM);
      Otherwise an ssh-style yes/no/[fingerprint] prompt on a terminal
      Fail-closed when non-interactive.
    
      Provision + launch:
      Accepted keys are written to the source's ~/.ssh/known_hosts, constructed
      by us and fed to the remote via stdin. Then launch with StrictHostKeyChecking=yes.
    
      Add -4/-6 to force address family on every hop - keyscan, transfer, and
      launch ssh. -Y doesn't pass through to the keyscan so enabling it here makes
      sense and matches current practice. Eventually I want to make Happy Eyeballs
      available to any ssh process that connects to a remote. That's later work though.

